Create a concise, Git-ready Markdown triage and investigation guide for one detection.

INPUT
- Screenshot of the Splunk detection logic (attached). It contains only the SPL, with
  no detection name or links.

HEADER VALUES (never leave any of them blank)
- Detection Name: write exactly DETECTION_NAME
- DaC Link: write exactly DAC_LINK
- MITRE ATT&CK ID and Technique Name: identify the most likely technique from the
  logic in the screenshot. Use the official MITRE ATT&CK ID and name. For
  sub-techniques use "Parent Technique: Sub-technique". If the technique cannot be
  determined, write TECHNIQUE_ID and TECHNIQUE_NAME.

Treat this detection as new and separate: never reuse content from earlier guides in
this chat. Base the Detection Logic summary and the artifacts you reference only on
the logic shown in the screenshot. Do not make up field names, indexes or lookups.

AUDIENCE
Experienced Incident Response analysts. Keep it practical and concise. Do not give
long explanations.

AVAILABLE ANALYST TOOLS
- Splunk for host and network event logs
- Trellix Endpoint Security and Redline for endpoint triage and browser-history acquisition
- CrowdStrike Falcon, including Real-Time Response
- Out-of-band Windows and Linux VMs with open-source static and dynamic file-analysis tools

CONTENT REQUIREMENTS
1. Follow the template below exactly.
2. Write suspicious or malicious artifacts as angle-bracket variables, such as
   `<hostname>`, `<username>`, `<process_name>`, `<command_line>`, `<file_path>`,
   `<file_hash>`, `<remote_domain>`, `<remote_ip_address>`, `<malicious_url>`.
   Do not use real atomic IOCs (URLs, hashes, IPs, domains, filenames, paths,
   usernames, hostnames). Native OS binaries, built-in commands, Event IDs, and field
   names or lookups shown in the screenshot may be named.
3. Make every triage and investigation action specific to the technique.
4. Include validation steps for identifying approved or expected activity.
5. Include Splunk, Trellix/Redline, CrowdStrike Falcon and out-of-band analysis
   actions where they fit.
6. Include containment and escalation only when malicious activity is confirmed or
   strongly suspected.
7. Include realistic known false positives and malicious behaviors specific to the
   technique.
8. Include one concise example that contains no atomic IOCs.

FORMATTING RULES
- Text inside {{ }} in the template is an instruction. Replace it with real content.
  Never output the {{ }}, the instruction text, or square brackets around values.
- Wrap every angle-bracket variable, command, field name and lookup in backticks.
  GitHub removes a bare <variable> as HTML, so it would disappear.
- Write checklist items exactly as "- [ ] " with no leading spaces.
- Put one blank line before and after every heading. Do not put blank lines
  between items in the same list.
- Do not use HTML tags, emojis or horizontal rules.
- Output the finished guide inside a single ````markdown code block, with nothing
  before or after it.

